<section xml:id="chapter5_designing-a-simple-class">
    <title>Designing a Simple Class</title>

    <p>
        A class combines data and the functions that operate on that data.
        Designing a class means deciding what information an object should
        store, what operations it should provide, and which parts should be
        accessible from outside the class.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection_counter_class">
        <title>Designing a <c>Counter</c> Class</title>

        <p>
            Suppose we want to represent a counter. A counter should store a
            number and provide operations for increasing, decreasing, and
            reading that number.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_simple_counter"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Counter {
private:
    int count = 0;

public:
    void increase_count() {
        ++count;
    }

    void decrease_count() {
        --count;
    }

    int get_count() const {
        return count;
    }
};

int main() {
    Counter counter;

    counter.increase_count();
    counter.increase_count();
    counter.decrease_count();

    std::cout << counter.get_count() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            The <c>Counter</c> object owns its <c>count</c> data. Code outside
            the class cannot directly modify <c>count</c> because it is
            <c>private</c>. Instead, outside code uses the public member
            functions.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection_access_specifiers">
        <title>Access Specifiers</title>

        <p>
            An access specifier determines which parts of a class can be used
            from outside the class.
        </p>

        <dl>
            <li>
                <title><c>private</c></title>
                <p>
                    Private members can be accessed only by member functions
                    of the same class.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <title><c>public</c></title>
                <p>
                    Public members can be accessed by code outside the class.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>

        <p>
            A class usually keeps its data members private and makes selected
            operations public. This is an example of
            <term>encapsulation</term>.
        </p>

        <p>
            Encapsulation means keeping an object's data and the operations
            that manage that data together while controlling access to the
            data.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection_class_constructors">
        <title>Constructors</title>

        <p>
            A constructor is a special member function that runs automatically
            when an object is created. A constructor has the same name as its
            class and has no return type.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_counter_constructor"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Counter {
private:
    int count;

public:
    Counter() : count{0} {
    }

    void increase_count() {
        ++count;
    }

    int get_count() const {
        return count;
    }
};

int main() {
    Counter counter;

    counter.increase_count();

    std::cout << counter.get_count() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            When <c>Counter counter;</c> executes, C++ automatically calls
            <c>Counter()</c>, which initializes <c>count</c> to zero.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection_constructor_parameters">
        <title>Constructors with Parameters</title>

        <p>
            A constructor can accept arguments so that objects begin with
            different values.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_counter_parameter_constructor"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Counter {
private:
    int count;

public:
    Counter(int starting_count)
        : count{starting_count} {
    }

    void increase_count() {
        ++count;
    }

    int get_count() const {
        return count;
    }
};

int main() {
    Counter first_counter{0};
    Counter second_counter{10};

    first_counter.increase_count();

    std::cout << first_counter.get_count() << '\n';
    std::cout << second_counter.get_count() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection_member_initializer_lists">
        <title>Member-Initializer Lists</title>

        <p>
            The part after a constructor's parameter list and before its body
            is called a <term>member-initializer list</term>.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_member_initializer_list"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
class Counter {
private:
    int count;

public:
    Counter(int starting_count)
        : count{starting_count} {
    }
};
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            The member-initializer list directly initializes data members
            before the constructor body runs. It is the preferred way to
            initialize data members in a constructor.
        </p>

        <p>
            Member-initializer lists are especially important for
            <c>const</c> data members, reference data members, and data
            members that do not have a default constructor.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection_default_member_initializers">
        <title>Default Member Initializers</title>

        <p>
            A data member can specify a default member initializer directly in
            the class definition.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_default_member_initializer"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Counter {
private:
    int count = 0;

public:
    Counter() = default;

    Counter(int starting_count)
        : count{starting_count} {
    }

    int get_count() const {
        return count;
    }
};

int main() {
    Counter first_counter;
    Counter second_counter{25};

    std::cout << first_counter.get_count() << '\n';
    std::cout << second_counter.get_count() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            The default member initializer supplies a value when another
            constructor does not provide one. A constructor's
            member-initializer takes precedence over the default member
            initializer.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection_rectangle_class">
        <title>A Class with Several Operations</title>

        <p>
            Consider a class that represents a rectangle. A rectangle has a
            width and height, and it can calculate its area and perimeter.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_rectangle_class"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Rectangle {
private:
    double width;
    double height;

public:
    Rectangle(double rectangle_width, double rectangle_height)
        : width{rectangle_width}, height{rectangle_height} {
    }

    double calculate_area() const {
        return width * height;
    }

    double calculate_perimeter() const {
        return 2 * (width + height);
    }
};

int main() {
    Rectangle rectangle{4.0, 3.0};

    std::cout << rectangle.calculate_area() << '\n';
    std::cout << rectangle.calculate_perimeter() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection_protecting_invariants">
        <title>Keeping an Object Valid</title>

        <p>
            A class can protect rules about valid data. For example, a
            rectangle should not have a negative width or height. Such a rule
            is called an <term>invariant</term>.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_rectangle_invariant"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

class Rectangle {
private:
    double width;
    double height;

public:
    Rectangle(double rectangle_width, double rectangle_height)
        : width{rectangle_width}, height{rectangle_height} {
        if (width < 0 || height < 0) {
            throw std::invalid_argument(
                "Rectangle dimensions cannot be negative"
            );
        }
    }

    double calculate_area() const {
        return width * height;
    }
};

int main() {
    try {
        Rectangle rectangle{4.0, 3.0};

        std::cout << rectangle.calculate_area() << '\n';
    }
    catch (const std::invalid_argument& error) {
        std::cout << error.what() << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection_struct_and_class">
        <title><c>struct</c> and <c>class</c></title>

        <p>
            C++ provides two common ways to define a user-defined type:
            <c>struct</c> and <c>class</c>.
        </p>

        <p>
            These two forms have almost the same capabilities. The important
            default difference is access.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_struct_default_access"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
struct ExampleStruct {
    int value;  // public by default
};

class ExampleClass {
    int value;  // private by default
};
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            Both types can have data members, constructors, overloaded
            constructors, default member initializers, member functions,
            access specifiers, and member-initializer lists.
        </p>

        <itemizedlist>
            <listitem>
                <p>
                    A <c>struct</c> has public member access by default.
                </p>
            </listitem>

            <listitem>
                <p>
                    A <c>class</c> has private member access by default.
                </p>
            </listitem>

            <listitem>
                <p>
                    A <c>struct</c> also defaults to public inheritance.
                </p>
            </listitem>

            <listitem>
                <p>
                    A <c>class</c> defaults to private inheritance.
                </p>
            </listitem>
        </itemizedlist>

        <p>
            The inheritance default is important when inheritance is studied
            later. For the simple classes in this chapter, the primary
            practical difference is the default member access.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection_struct_constructor">
        <title>Structs Can Have Constructors and Member Functions</title>

        <p>
            A <c>struct</c> is not limited to storing data. It can have
            constructors and member functions just like a class.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_struct_constructor"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

struct Point {
    int x;
    int y;

    Point(int point_x, int point_y)
        : x{point_x}, y{point_y} {
    }

    void move_right() {
        ++x;
    }
};

int main() {
    Point point{3, 4};

    point.move_right();

    std::cout << point.x << '\n';
    std::cout << point.y << '\n';

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection_struct_overloaded_constructors">
        <title>Structs Can Have Overloaded Constructors</title>

        <program xml:id="program_struct_overloaded_constructors"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

struct Point {
    int x = 0;
    int y = 0;

    Point() = default;

    Point(int point_x, int point_y)
        : x{point_x}, y{point_y} {
    }
};

int main() {
    Point origin;
    Point point{3, 4};

    std::cout << origin.x << ", " << origin.y << '\n';
    std::cout << point.x << ", " << point.y << '\n';

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection_struct_private_members">
        <title>Structs Can Have Private Members</title>

        <p>
            A struct can use <c>public</c>, <c>private</c>, and
            <c>protected</c> access specifiers just like a class.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_struct_private_members"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

struct Temperature {
private:
    double celsius;

public:
    Temperature(double starting_celsius)
        : celsius{starting_celsius} {
    }

    double get_celsius() const {
        return celsius;
    }
};

int main() {
    Temperature temperature{22.5};

    std::cout << temperature.get_celsius() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection_struct_class_design_choice">
        <title>Choosing Between <c>struct</c> and <c>class</c></title>

        <p>
            In C++, <c>struct</c> and <c>class</c> have nearly the same
            capabilities. The choice is primarily a design and communication
            decision.
        </p>

        <p>
            A <c>struct</c> often communicates that a type is a simple
            collection of related values and that public access is appropriate.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_data_oriented_struct"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

struct StudentRecord {
    std::string name;
    int student_id;
};

int main() {
    StudentRecord student{"Amina", 1042};

    std::cout << student.name << '\n';
    std::cout << student.student_id << '\n';

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            A <c>class</c> often communicates that a type should protect its
            data and provide operations through member functions.
        </p>

        <program xml:id="program_encapsulated_class"
                 language="cpp"
                 interactive="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class StudentAccount {
private:
    double balance = 0.0;

public:
    void deposit_money(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0) {
            balance += amount;
        }
    }

    double get_balance() const {
        return balance;
    }
};

int main() {
    StudentAccount account;

    account.deposit_money(100.00);
    account.deposit_money(25.50);

    std::cout << account.get_balance() << '\n';

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>

        <p>
            This is a convention, not a restriction. A struct can be complex
            and encapsulated, and a class can contain public data.
        </p>

        <p>
            Use a <c>struct</c> when the type is mainly a group of related
            values and public access is part of the intended interface. Use a
            <c>class</c> when the type should protect its data, enforce
            invariants, or provide behavior through member functions.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection_struct_class_summary">
        <title>Summary of <c>struct</c> and <c>class</c></title>

        <p>
            The following table summarizes the relationship between
            <c>struct</c> and <c>class</c>.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_struct_class_comparison">
            <title><c>struct</c> and <c>class</c> comparison</title>

            <tabular>
                <row>
                    <cell>Feature</cell>
                    <cell><c>struct</c></cell>
                    <cell><c>class</c></cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Default member access</cell>
                    <cell><c>public</c></cell>
                    <cell><c>private</c></cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Data members</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Constructors</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Overloaded constructors</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Default member initializers</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Member functions</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Access specifiers</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Member-initializer lists</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Inheritance</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                    <cell>Yes</cell>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>Default inheritance access</cell>
                    <cell><c>public</c></cell>
                    <cell><c>private</c></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            The main language difference between a struct and a class is their
            default access. The broader choice is mainly about communicating
            design intent to other programmers.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsection_designing_class_summary">
        <title>Summary</title>

        <ul>
            <li>
                A class combines data and operations into a user-defined type.
            </li>
            <li>
                Constructors initialize objects when they are created.
            </li>
            <li>
                Constructors can receive arguments and can be overloaded.
            </li>
            <li>
                A member-initializer list directly initializes data members.
            </li>
            <li>
                Default member initializers provide fallback values.
            </li>
            <li>
                Private members protect an object's internal data.
            </li>
            <li>
                Public member functions define how outside code interacts with
                an object.
            </li>
            <li>
                Both structs and classes can have constructors, overloaded
                constructors, default member initializers, member functions,
                access specifiers, and member-initializer lists.
            </li>
            <li>
                Structs default to public member access.
            </li>
            <li>
                Classes default to private member access.
            </li>
            <li>
                Choosing <c>struct</c> or <c>class</c> is mainly a design and
                communication decision.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </subsection>
</section>
